      r >= 2 is 'n/a' BY CONSTRUCTION, not by cost: the volume-averaged
      object exists only on the contact shell. That is not the limitation
      it first appears, because contact is exactly where the midpoint rule
      fails -- beyond it (cell size)/(separation) < 1 and falls, so the
      point propagator is already good there.

      WITHDRAWN: this section previously concluded that 'Galerkin
      averaging at contact LOWERS the error but does NOT restore
      convergence -- it still grows with refinement'. That was measured
      on the TRUNCATED lateral sum, whose own growth dominated and which
      no contact fix could have removed. On the exact lattice sum the
      r = 1 column is FLAT. The growth is gone.

========================================================================================
  WHAT THE RE-RUN SHOWS. The non-convergence had TWO causes, and the
  T-matrix was neither:

    1. the TRUNCATED lateral sum. Its artifact ACCUMULATED plane by
       plane, which is why the error grew with n_z instead of merely
       sitting at a floor -- the question left open when the defect was
       found. Exact summation cuts the n_z = 16 error from 2.62e-2 to
       5.06e-3 and turns the ratio from 1.29, still climbing, into 1.05.
    2. the CONTACT MIDPOINT RULE. Adding the Galerkin contact correction
       to the exact sum flattens it outright: 7.73e-4, 8.35e-4, 8.51e-4,
       8.58e-4 across n_z = 1, 2, 4, 8.

  SO THE SCHEME CONVERGES. What it converges TO is not zero: a residual
  of about 8.6e-4 survives refinement. That is the genuine scale-
  invariant per-cell error and it is now the only thing left to explain
  -- with the same two suspects as before, but facing a flat curve
  rather than a growing one:
    (a) the averaged object's dynamic content is truncated at O(w^4);
    (b) a space-filling lattice of ISOLATED-cube T-matrices may need a
        lattice renormalisation to reproduce the continuum -- the same
        class of correction as the sphere-packing Delta -> Delta/phi
        already established in this project.

  'ratio' is the error multiplier per refinement step, each step HALVING
  the cell size. Ratio ~ 1 is a scale-invariant per-cell error; ratio > 1
  means the error is also ACCUMULATING over cells, which is what the
  truncated column still shows and the exact one no longer does.

  'T nonlin' differs by 1000x between the two contrast arms while the
  ratio columns do not, so the T-matrix's nonlinear content is exonerated
  -- as before, but now without a confounded kernel underneath it.
========================================================================================
